[file_ops]
**## File Operations ##**

All file operations are available under the **File** menu (**Alt+F**).

* **New** (**Ctrl+N**): Creates a new, empty buffer.

* **Open** (**Ctrl+O**): Opens the refined file browser. 
    - **Iconic List**: Directories are marked with `[DIR]` and files with `[FIL]`.
    - **Details Panel**: Shows type, owner, permissions, size, and modification date.
    - **Navigation**: Use **Backspace** to quickly go up to the parent directory.
    - **Smart Input**: Type a filename directly or select it from the list.

* **Save** (**Ctrl+S**): Saves the current file.

* **Save As...**: Opens the file browser to save the current buffer to a new name.

* **Exit** (**Alt+X**): Closes the editor. 
    - **Safety First**: Gedi will automatically scan all open buffers and prompt you to save any unsaved changes before exiting.

Return to [[main|Main Menu]].

[editing]
**## Editing and Selection ##**

Standard text editing is enhanced with clipboard operations, multi-level undo/redo, and a powerful comment toggle. Most features are in the **Edit** menu (**Alt+E**).

* **Undo** (**Alt+Backspace**): Reverts the last change.
* **Redo** (**Alt+Y**): Re-applies an action that was undone.
* **Cut/Copy/Paste** (**Ctrl+X**, **Ctrl+C**, **Ctrl+V**): Uses the system clipboard for integration with other applications.

**Selection:**
Text can be selected using **Shift + Arrow Keys**. Press **Escape** to clear selection.

**Toggle Comment** (**Ctrl+/**):
* Toggles `//` comments on the current line or the entire selected block.

Return to [[main|Main Menu]].

[navigation]
**## Navigation and Search ##**

**Cursor Movement:**
* **Ctrl+Left/Right**: Move by word.
* **Home/End**: Jump to line start or end.
* **Page Up/Down**: Scroll by full pages.

**Incremental Search:**
* **Ctrl+F**: Opens the search bar at the bottom of the screen.
    - Start typing to search in real time (triggers after 2 characters).
    - Press **Enter** to find the next occurrence.
    - Press **Escape** to close the search bar.

**Find Next / Find Previous:**
* Available in the **Search** menu (**Alt+S**) as **Find Next** and **Find Previous**.
* These repeat the last search forward or backward without reopening the search bar.

**Search and Replace:**
* **Ctrl+R**: Opens the **Replace** dialog. See [[replace|Search and Replace]] for details.

**Go To Line:**
* Jump directly to a specific line number via **Alt+S -> G**.

Return to [[main|Main Menu]].

[building]
**## Compiling and Running ##**

Gedi uses automatic build detection via an integrated build system and `cguess.py`.

* **Compile Only** (**Shift+F9**): Builds the current file and shows the output.
* **Compile and Run** (**F9**): Builds and immediately executes the program if successful.

**Build Output:**
If compilation fails, the output window will highlight errors. You can navigate through the output and press **Enter** on an error message to jump directly to that line in your code.

Return to [[main|Main Menu]].

[compiler_options]
**## Compiler Options Dialog ##**

The redesigned Compiler Options dialog (**Alt+B -> O**) provides a powerful interface for configuring your build process.

* **C++ Standard**: Select your desired language version (c++11 through c++26) from the dropdown at the top.
* **Tabbed Categories**: Switches are organized into three tabs:
    - **Basic**: Essential flags for optimization and standard warnings.
    - **Advanced**: Professional diagnostics, sanitizers (ASan/UBSan), and LTO.
    - **Expert**: Situational flags, functionality controls (exceptions/RTTI), and linker options.
* **Final Command**: A live preview of the full command that will be sent to the compiler. This field is scrollable using arrow keys.
* **Copy to Clipboard**: Use the dedicated **Copy** button to quickly export the generated command for use in terminals or scripts.

**Note**: Settings are saved **per-buffer**, allowing different configuration for each open file.

Return to [[main|Main Menu]] or view [[compiler_switches|Detailed Switches]].

[compiler_switches]
**## Detailed Compiler Switches Index ##**

**### Basic Switches ###**
[[sw_g|-g : Generate debug symbols]]
[[sw_O0|-O0 : Disable optimisation]]
[[sw_O2|-O2 : Balanced optimisation]]
[[sw_O3|-O3 : High optimisation]]
[[sw_Wall|-Wall : Enable common warnings]]
[[sw_Wextra|-Wextra : Enable extra warnings]]
[[sw_Wpedantic|-Wpedantic : Strict ISO compliance]]
[[sw_Werror|-Werror : Treat warnings as errors]]

**### Advanced Switches ###**
[[sw_Wconversion|-Wconversion : Implicit conversion warnings]]
[[sw_Wsign_conversion|-Wsign-conversion : Signed/unsigned conv warnings]]
[[sw_Wshadow|-Wshadow : Variable shadowing warnings]]
[[sw_Wnon_virtual_dtor|-Wnon-virtual-dtor : Polymorphic class warnings]]
[[sw_Wold_style_cast|-Wold-style-cast : C-style cast warnings]]
[[sw_Woverloaded_virtual|-Woverloaded-virtual : Virtual function shadowing]]
[[sw_Wnull_dereference|-Wnull-dereference : Null pointer checks]]
[[sw_Wdouble_promotion|-Wdouble-promotion : Float promotion warnings]]
[[sw_Wformat_2|-Wformat=2 : Strict format string checking]]
[[sw_fno_omit_fp|-fno-omit-frame-pointer : Preservation of frame pointers]]
[[sw_fsanitize_au|-fsanitize=address,undefined : ASan and UBSan]]
[[sw_fsanitize_leak|-fsanitize=leak : LeakSanitizer]]
[[sw_flto|-flto : Link-time optimisation]]
[[sw_march_native|-march=native : Optimise for host CPU]]
[[sw_mtune_native|-mtune=native : Tune for host CPU]]

**### Expert Switches ###**
[[sw_Wcast_align|-Wcast-align : Cast alignment warnings]]
[[sw_Wcast_qual|-Wcast-qual : Cast qualifier warnings]]
[[sw_Wswitch_enum|-Wswitch-enum : Missing enum case warnings]]
[[sw_Wundef|-Wundef : Undefined macro warnings]]
[[sw_Wredundant_decls|-Wredundant-decls : Redundant declarations]]
[[sw_Wlogical_op|-Wlogical-op : Logical operation warnings]]
[[sw_Wuseless_cast|-Wuseless-cast : Useless cast warnings]]
[[sw_Weffcxx|-Weffc++ : Effective C++ warnings]]
[[sw_fno_exceptions|-fno-exceptions : Disable exceptions]]
[[sw_fno_rtti|-fno-rtti : Disable RTTI]]
[[sw_fvisibility_hidden|-fvisibility=hidden : Hide symbols by default]]
[[sw_fstrict_aliasing|-fstrict-aliasing : Enable strict aliasing]]
[[sw_fsanitize_pc|-fsanitize=pointer-compare : Pointer comparison checks]]
[[sw_fsanitize_ps|-fsanitize=pointer-subtract : Pointer arithmetic checks]]
[[sw_Wl_as_needed|-Wl,--as-needed : Remove unused dependencies]]
[[sw_Wl_O1|-Wl,-O1 : Optimise linking]]

Return to [[main|Main Menu]].

[sw_g]
**-g : Generate debug symbols**

Embeds debugging information in the compiled binary. This is required for using debuggers like `gdb` or `lldb`. It maps the machine code back to your source code, enabling breakpoints and variable inspection.

Return to [[compiler_switches|Switches Index]].

[sw_O0]
**-O0 : Disable optimisation**

Completely disables compiler optimisations. The generated machine code will strictly follow your source code, making it the best choice for debugging as variable values and execution flow remain predictable.

Return to [[compiler_switches|Switches Index]].

[sw_O2]
**-O2 : Balanced optimisation**

Enables a wide range of optimisations that increase performance without significant increases in binary size or compilation time. This is the standard recommended level for most production builds.

Return to [[compiler_switches|Switches Index]].

[sw_O3]
**-O3 : High optimisation**

Enables aggressive optimisations, including loop vectorisation and deeper inlining. While it can result in faster code, it may increase binary size and occasionally introduce subtle bugs if your code relies on undefined behaviour.

Return to [[compiler_switches|Switches Index]].

[sw_Wall]
**-Wall : Enable common warnings**

Enables a broad set of warnings that catch common programming errors, such as unused variables, uninitialised variables, and missing return statements. This is considered the minimum safe level for any C++ project.

Return to [[compiler_switches|Switches Index]].

[sw_Wextra]
**-Wextra : Enable extra warnings**

Enables additional warnings not covered by `-Wall`. This catches more subtle issues like signed/unsigned comparisons, unused parameters, and potential logic errors in nested conditions.

Return to [[compiler_switches|Switches Index]].

[sw_Wpedantic]
**-Wpedantic : Strict ISO compliance**

Enforces strict adherence to the ISO C++ standard. It issues warnings for any use of compiler-specific extensions or non-standard syntax, ensuring your code is as portable as possible.

Return to [[compiler_switches|Switches Index]].

[sw_Werror]
**-Werror : Treat warnings as errors**

Tells the compiler to treat every warning as a hard compilation error. This ensures that no potential issues are ignored and that the codebase remains clean and warning-free.

Return to [[compiler_switches|Switches Index]].

[sw_Wconversion]
**-Wconversion : Implicit conversion warnings**

Warns about implicit type conversions that may alter a value or lose precision, such as assigning a `double` to an `int` or a large integer type to a smaller one.

Return to [[compiler_switches|Switches Index]].

[sw_Wsign_conversion]
**-Wsign-conversion : Signed/unsigned conv warnings**

Specifically warns about implicit conversions between signed and unsigned integer types. These conversions can often lead to unexpected behaviour and subtle logic bugs.

Return to [[compiler_switches|Switches Index]].

[sw_Wshadow]
**-Wshadow : Variable shadowing warnings**

Warns when a local variable hides (shadows) another variable, parameter, or member with the same name in an outer scope. This helps prevent confusing logic errors.

Return to [[compiler_switches|Switches Index]].

[sw_Wnon_virtual_dtor]
**-Wnon-virtual-dtor : Polymorphic class warnings**

Warns when a class has virtual functions but no virtual destructor. This is dangerous because deleting a derived object via a base class pointer will result in undefined behaviour.

Return to [[compiler_switches|Switches Index]].

[sw_Wold_style_cast]
**-Wold-style-cast : C-style cast warnings**

Warns when a C-style cast is used. In C++, it is safer and clearer to use `static_cast`, `reinterpret_cast`, or `const_cast` to make the programmer's intent explicit.

Return to [[compiler_switches|Switches Index]].

[sw_Woverloaded_virtual]
**-Woverloaded-virtual : Virtual function shadowing**

Warns when a function declaration in a derived class hides a virtual function in a base class because the signatures do not match exactly.

Return to [[compiler_switches|Switches Index]].

[sw_Wnull_dereference]
**-Wnull-dereference : Null pointer checks**

Enables static analysis to detect potential null pointer dereferences. While it cannot catch every case, it helps identify common paths where a null pointer might be accessed.

Return to [[compiler_switches|Switches Index]].

[sw_Wdouble_promotion]
**-Wdouble_promotion : Float promotion warnings**

Warns when a `float` value is implicitly promoted to `double`. This is often unintentional in performance-critical code, especially when targeting hardware with specific single-precision optimisations.

Return to [[compiler_switches|Switches Index]].

[sw_Wformat_2]
**-Wformat=2 : Strict format string checking**

Provides more rigorous checking of `printf`/`scanf` style format strings, including checks for potential security vulnerabilities and platform-specific quirks.

Return to [[compiler_switches|Switches Index]].

[sw_fno_omit_fp]
**-fno-omit-frame-pointer : Preservation of frame pointers**

Forces the compiler to keep the frame pointer in a register. This makes stack traces more reliable and improves the accuracy of profiling tools, though it may slightly reduce performance.

Return to [[compiler_switches|Switches Index]].

[sw_fsanitize_au]
**-fsanitize=address,undefined : ASan and UBSan**

Enables the AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer. These instruments your code to detect memory errors (like buffer overflows) and undefined behaviour (like null pointer dereferences) at runtime.

Return to [[compiler_switches|Switches Index]].

[sw_fsanitize_leak]
**-fsanitize=leak : LeakSanitizer**

Enables the LeakSanitizer, which detects memory leaks at the end of program execution. This is extremely useful for ensuring all allocated resources are properly cleaned up.

Return to [[compiler_switches|Switches Index]].

[sw_flto]
**-flto : Link-time optimisation**

Enables optimisation across different source files at link time. This allows the compiler to perform inlining and dead-code elimination more effectively, often resulting in significant performance gains.

Return to [[compiler_switches|Switches Index]].

[sw_march_native]
**-march=native : Optimise for host CPU**

Tells the compiler to generate machine code specifically for the architecture of the machine on which it is currently running. This enables all instruction set extensions (like AVX or NEON) supported by your CPU.

Return to [[compiler_switches|Switches Index]].

[sw_mtune_native]
**-mtune=native : Tune for host CPU**

Optimises the generated code for the host CPU's specific pipeline and cache characteristics without requiring instruction set extensions. This is safer than `-march=native` if the binary needs to run on older CPUs.

Return to [[compiler_switches|Switches Index]].

[sw_Wcast_align]
**-Wcast-align : Cast alignment warnings**

Warns whenever a pointer is cast such that the required alignment of the target is increased. This is especially important for architectures that do not support unaligned memory access.

Return to [[compiler_switches|Switches Index]].

[sw_Wcast_qual]
**-Wcast-qual : Cast qualifier warnings**

Warns whenever a pointer is cast so as to remove a type qualifier from the target type (such as casting a `const char *` to a `char *`).

Return to [[compiler_switches|Switches Index]].

[sw_Wswitch_enum]
**-Wswitch-enum : Missing enum case warnings**

Warns whenever a `switch` statement on an enum type does not handle all possible values of that enum, even if a `default` case is present.

Return to [[compiler_switches|Switches Index]].

[sw_Wundef]
**-Wundef : Undefined macro warnings**

Warns if an undefined identifier is evaluated in an `#if` or `#elif` preprocessor directive. This catches typos in macro names.

Return to [[compiler_switches|Switches Index]].

[sw_Wredundant_decls]
**-Wredundant-decls : Redundant declarations**

Warns if anything is declared more than once in the same scope, even if the declarations are consistent. This helps keep headers clean.

Return to [[compiler_switches|Switches Index]].

[sw_Wlogical_op]
**-Wlogical-op : Logical operation warnings**

Warns about suspicious uses of logical operators, such as constant results or redundant expressions (e.g., `x < 5 && x < 10`).

Return to [[compiler_switches|Switches Index]].

[sw_Wuseless_cast]
**-Wuseless-cast : Useless cast warnings**

Warns when the source and target types of a cast are identical, indicating that the cast is unnecessary and could be removed.

Return to [[compiler_switches|Switches Index]].

[sw_Weffcxx]
**-Weffc++ : Effective C++ warnings**

Enables a set of warnings based on Scott Meyers' *Effective C++* books. It catches common design flaws in object-oriented C++ code, such as missing copy constructors or assignment operators.

Return to [[compiler_switches|Switches Index]].

[sw_fno_exceptions]
**-fno-exceptions : Disable exceptions**

Disables the generation of exception handling code. This results in smaller and potentially faster binaries but means you cannot use `try`, `catch`, or `throw` in your code.

Return to [[compiler_switches|Switches Index]].

[sw_fno_rtti]
**-fno-rtti : Disable RTTI**

Disables Run-Time Type Information. This removes support for `dynamic_cast` and `typeid`, which can significantly reduce binary size in systems with many polymorphic classes.

Return to [[compiler_switches|Switches Index]].

[sw_fvisibility_hidden]
**-fvisibility=hidden : Hide symbols by default**

Changes the default visibility of symbols to "hidden". This is used when building shared libraries to ensure only explicitly exported symbols are accessible from outside the library.

Return to [[compiler_switches|Switches Index]].

[sw_fstrict_aliasing]
**-fstrict-aliasing : Enable strict aliasing**

Allows the compiler to assume that pointers to different types do not point to the same memory location. This allows for aggressive optimisations but can break code that violates the strict aliasing rule.

Return to [[compiler_switches|Switches Index]].

[sw_fsanitize_pc]
**-fsanitize=pointer-compare : Pointer comparison checks**

Instrumental check that warns about comparisons between pointers that do not point to the same object (e.g., comparing pointers to two different arrays).

Return to [[compiler_switches|Switches Index]].

[sw_fsanitize_ps]
**-fsanitize=pointer-subtract : Pointer arithmetic checks**

Instrumental check that warns about subtraction of pointers that do not point to the same object.

Return to [[compiler_switches|Switches Index]].

[sw_Wl_as_needed]
**-Wl,--as-needed : Remove unused dependencies**

Tells the linker to only add a `DT_NEEDED` entry for a dynamic library if it is actually used by the binary. This reduces the number of runtime dependencies.

Return to [[compiler_switches|Switches Index]].

[sw_Wl_O1]
**-Wl,-O1 : Optimise linking**

Enables basic linker-level optimisations, such as removing redundant information and slightly improving the layout of the final binary.

Return to [[compiler_switches|Switches Index]].

[replace]
**## Search and Replace ##**

Press **Ctrl+R** (or use **Search -> Replace** in the menu) to open the Replace dialog.

The dialog has two fields:
* **Search**: The text to find.
* **Replace**: The text to substitute in its place.

**Actions:**
* **Replace**: Finds the next occurrence of the search term from the current cursor position and replaces it. Running Replace again moves to the next match.
* **Replace All**: Replaces every occurrence in the entire file at once. A message will confirm how many substitutions were made.

**Notes:**
* The Replace dialog is not available on read-only buffers.
* The search term is shared with the incremental search bar (**Ctrl+F**), so it persists between the two.

Return to [[main|Main Menu]] or see [[navigation|Navigation and Search]].

[project_mgmt]
**## Project Management ##**

Gedi has a built-in project system for managing multi-file C++ builds. Projects are stored in a `.gproj` file in the project's root directory.

**Creating a Project:**
* Open the **Project** menu and choose **New Project**.
* A dialog will guide you through setting the project name, root directory, build system, C++ standard, compiler flags, and any linked libraries.
* Gedi supports three build systems: **cmake**, **make**, and **meson**. After confirming, it generates the corresponding build file (`CMakeLists.txt`, `Makefile`, or `meson.build`) automatically.

**Opening a Project:**
* Choose **Project -> Open Project** and select the `.gproj` file.
* The project's sources are then shown in the [[project_panel|Project Panel]].

**Closing a Project:**
* Choose **Project -> Close Project** to unload the current project.

**Adding Files:**
* Use **Project -> Add File to Project** to include the currently open file in a project target.
* Gedi updates the build file on disk immediately.

**Library Scanning:**
* When creating or editing a project, Gedi scans the system for available development libraries and presents them in a searchable list so you can link them without manually specifying flags.

Return to [[main|Main Menu]] or see [[project_panel|Project Panel]].

[project_panel]
**## Project Panel ##**

The Project Panel is a side panel that displays your project's structure and lets you navigate its files quickly.

**Toggling the Panel:**
* Press **Alt+0** to show or hide the Project Panel.
* The panel requires an open project; it will show a message if no project is loaded.

**Panel Structure:**
The panel shows three levels:
1. **Build file** — the generated `CMakeLists.txt`, `Makefile`, or `meson.build`.
2. **Targets** — each executable or library defined in the project.
3. **Source files** — the `.cpp` and `.h` files belonging to each target.

**Navigation:**
* **Up/Down arrow keys**: Move the cursor through the list.
* **Enter**: Open the highlighted file in the editor.
* **Tab**: Move focus back to the editor without closing the panel.
* **Escape**: Close the Project Panel.

**Editing the Project:**
* **Del**: Remove the selected item. Selecting a source file removes it from its target (with confirmation). Selecting a target removes it and all its sources (with confirmation). The build file cannot be removed.
* **M**: Move the selected source file to a different target. A picker lets you choose the destination target.

All changes are saved to the `.gproj` file and the build file on disk immediately.

Return to [[main|Main Menu]] or see [[project_mgmt|Project Management]].

[goto_def]
**## Go to Definition ##**

Press **F12** to jump to the definition of the symbol under the cursor.

**How it works:**
* Gedi uses **libclang** to parse the current file and resolve the symbol at the cursor position to its definition.
* If the definition is in another file, that file is opened in a new buffer automatically and the cursor is placed on the correct line.

**Spinner:**
* Parsing a large file can take a moment. While working, the status bar displays a spinner and the name of the symbol being searched.
* Press **Escape** to cancel a lookup that is taking too long.

**Fast path:**
* If the symbol was already resolved in a previous parse, Gedi can jump immediately without a full re-parse.

**Notes:**
* Go to Definition works best on C and C++ files that are part of a project with a properly configured include path.
* It requires libclang to be installed on the system.

Return to [[main|Main Menu]].

[syntax_hl]
**## Syntax Highlighting ##**

Gedi automatically detects the language of a file from its name or extension and applies appropriate syntax highlighting.

**Supported file types:**

* **C / C++**: `.c`, `.h`, `.cpp`, `.hpp`, `.cxx`
    - Keywords, preprocessor directives, string literals, numbers, comments, and libclang-powered semantic token coloring.

* **CMake**: `CMakeLists.txt`
    - CMake commands, variables, generator expressions, and quoted strings.

* **Makefile**: `Makefile`, `GNUmakefile`
    - Targets, variables, automatic variables, and recipe lines.

* **Assembly**: `.s`, `.asm`
    - Mnemonics, registers, directives, labels, and numeric literals.

* **Linker Script**: `.ld`
    - Section keywords, memory regions, symbols, and expressions.

* **PRIMAL**: `.prim`
    - Syntax for the PRIMAL domain-specific language.

* **GLSL**: `.glsl`, `.vert`, `.frag`
    - GLSL keywords, built-in variables, type names, and preprocessor directives.

Files that do not match any of the above are displayed without highlighting.

Return to [[main|Main Menu]].

[windows]
**## Window Management ##**

Gedi supports multiple open files (buffers) simultaneously.

* **Next/Previous Window** (**F6** / **Shift+F6**): Cycle through open buffers.
* **Direct Access**: Use **Alt+1** through **Alt+9** to jump directly to a specific buffer.
* **Close Window** (**Alt+W** or **Ctrl+W**): Closes the current buffer. You will be prompted to save if changes exist.
* **Output Screen** (**F5**): Toggles the visibility of the compilation output.

Return to [[main|Main Menu]].

[options]
**## Editor Settings ##**

The **Editor Settings** dialog (**Alt+O -> S**) allows you to customize your environment:

* **Indentation**: Toggle Smart Indent and set your preferred Tab Size.
* **View**: Toggle the display of line numbers.
* **Color Scheme**: Select from various built-in themes to change the IDE's look and feel.

Internal groups in settings use single-line borders for a clean visual hierarchy.

Return to [[main|Main Menu]].

[hotkeys]
**## Quick Hotkey Reference ##**

**File & Editor**
Ctrl+N - New File        F3     - Open File
F2     - Save File       Alt+X  - Exit IDE
Alt+F3 - Close Window    F6     - Next Window
Shift+F6 - Prev Window

**Editing**
Ctrl+INS  - Copy         Shift+DEL - Cut
Shift+INS - Paste        Alt+BS    - Undo
Alt+Y     - Redo         Ctrl+/    - Toggle Comment
DEL       - Delete Fwd

**Search & Navigation**
Ctrl+F   - Find (inline)  Ctrl+R   - Replace Dialog
Alt+S->G - Go To Line     F12      - Go to Definition

**Project**
Alt+0    - Toggle Project Panel

**Build & Run**
Alt+F9   - Compile Only  Ctrl+F9  - Compile & Run
Alt+F5   - Toggle Output

Return to [[main|Main Menu]].
